http: what is set aside for another host is also sent when a redirect leaves TLS

`headers_elsewhere` states what to send in place of `headers` once a
redirect has led to a host of another name: the same headers less the
ones that say who is asking. A redirect on the same host was left
alone, whatever it did to the scheme.

One of those is not harmless. A server asked over HTTPS that answers
with a redirect to itself over plain HTTP has the token, the password
or the cookie that was written to go where nobody could read it sent
again where anybody on the way can. From HTTPS to HTTP on the same
host the headers set aside now take the place of `headers` too, and
keep it for the rest of the connection's life, as after a change of
host. From HTTP to HTTPS, and to another port, nothing changes.

--- a/libavformat/http.c	2026-11-14 15:19:21
+++ b/libavformat/http.c	2026-11-14 15:19:41
@@ -405,18 +405,23 @@
 
 /* Custom headers are written for the host that is asked. When that host
  * sends the request on to another one, what the caller set aside for other
- * hosts takes their place, for as long as this connection lasts. */
+ * hosts takes their place, for as long as this connection lasts. The same
+ * when it sends a request that was made over TLS on to itself without TLS:
+ * what was written to be sent where nobody could read it is not sent where
+ * anybody on the way can. */
 static int http_leave_host(HTTPContext *s, const char *from, const char *to)
 {
+    char proto_from[16], proto_to[16];
     char host_from[1024], host_to[1024];
     char *elsewhere;
     size_t len;
 
     if (!s->headers_elsewhere)
         return 0;
-    av_url_split(NULL, 0, NULL, 0, host_from, sizeof(host_from), NULL, NULL, 0, from);
-    av_url_split(NULL, 0, NULL, 0, host_to, sizeof(host_to), NULL, NULL, 0, to);
-    if (!av_strcasecmp(host_from, host_to))
+    av_url_split(proto_from, sizeof(proto_from), NULL, 0, host_from, sizeof(host_from), NULL, NULL, 0, from);
+    av_url_split(proto_to, sizeof(proto_to), NULL, 0, host_to, sizeof(host_to), NULL, NULL, 0, to);
+    if (!av_strcasecmp(host_from, host_to) &&
+        !(!av_strcasecmp(proto_from, "https") && !av_strcasecmp(proto_to, "http")))
         return 0;
 
     len = strlen(s->headers_elsewhere);
